An event-booking service counts ticket sales in a shared variable. Each of four seller threads records 100 000 sales:

```c
long sold = 0;
pthread_mutex_t m = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

void *seller(void *arg) {
    int id = *(int *)arg;
    printf("seller %d starting\n", id);
    for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++)
        sold++;                                /* version A */
    return NULL;
}

int main(void) {
    pthread_t t[4];
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++)
        pthread_create(&t[i], NULL, seller, &i);
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++)
        pthread_join(t[i], NULL);
    printf("sold = %ld\n", sold);
}
```

Two alternatives were also written. **Version B** wraps every `sold++` in `pthread_mutex_lock(&m)` / `unlock`. **Version C** counts in a local variable and adds it to `sold` once, under the mutex, after the loop. Measured on a 4-core machine, compiled with `gcc -O0 -pthread`:

| Version | Printed `sold` over five runs | Time |
|---|---|---|
| A | 271 904 · 312 477 · 288 015 · 301 552 · 266 830 | 0.002 s |
| B | 400 000 every run | 0.041 s |
| C | 400 000 every run | 0.0004 s |

The start-up lines sometimes read `seller 2 starting` twice and never `seller 0 starting`.

A print spooler shares a circular queue of capacity 8 between 3 workstation threads (producers) and 2 printer threads (consumers). One mutex `m` and **one** condition variable `changed` are used:

```c
void submit(job_t j) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&m);
    if (count == CAP)
        pthread_cond_wait(&changed, &m);
    queue[tail] = j;  tail = (tail + 1) % CAP;  count++;
    pthread_cond_signal(&changed);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&m);
}

job_t take(void) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&m);
    if (count == 0)
        pthread_cond_wait(&changed, &m);
    job_t j = queue[head];  head = (head + 1) % CAP;  count--;
    pthread_cond_signal(&changed);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&m);
    return j;
}
```

At the end each workstation submits one special `STOP` job; a printer exits when it takes a `STOP` job. In testing, two problems appear rarely: a printer occasionally prints a garbage job, and once the whole system froze with the queue full and every thread blocked.

A photo tool processes an image of `H` rows on a 4-core machine (compiled with `gcc -fopenmp -O0`):

```c
// Loop 1 - blur: every row costs the same
#pragma omp parallel for
for (int r = 0; r < H; r++) blur_row(r);

// Loop 2 - count bright pixels
int bright = 0;
#pragma omp parallel for
for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)
    if (img[i] > 200) bright++;

// Loop 3 - fractal preview: rows near the middle take ~50x longer than edge rows
#pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)
for (int r = 0; r < H; r++) render_row(r);

// Debug print
int level = 5;
#pragma omp parallel private(level)
printf("level = %d\n", level);
```

Loop 2 prints a different, too-small `bright` on every run. Three fixes were timed with 4 threads: `#pragma omp critical` 0.31 s, `#pragma omp atomic` 0.12 s, `reduction(+:bright)` 0.015 s. Loop 3 takes almost as long with 4 threads as with 1.

